Q: With PHPStan, how does one annotate a type as being a non-empty Collection? A: <p>In PHPStan, there is <strong>no built-in annotation for a “non-empty Collection”</strong> like there is for arrays. The recommended and simplest approach is <strong>type + runtime check</strong>.</p>
<p>You annotate the variable as a normal Collection, then explicitly check that it’s not empty. After that check, PHPStan understands it as non-empty.</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre><code>use Doctrine\Common\Collections\Collection;

/** @var Collection&lt;int, User&gt; $users */
$users = $group-&gt;getUsers();

if ($users-&gt;isEmpty()) {
    throw new RuntimeException('Collection is empty');
}

// From here on, PHPStan knows $users is non-empty
</code></pre>
<p>You can also use:</p>
<pre><code>assert(!$users-&gt;isEmpty());
</code></pre>
<p>if you don’t want to throw an exception. PHPStan recognizes <code>assert()</code> and refines the type.</p>
<p>For arrays only, PHPStan supports <code>non-empty-array</code>, but <strong>collections rely on runtime checks</strong>, not annotations.</p>
